00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.720
or continuously is
struggling with what do we

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:01.260
do with the data, specifically
with the unstructured data?

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:03.260
And is anybody in
the audience here

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.010
who works in the health
care specifically,

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.362
health care vertical?

00:04:06.362 --> 00:04:07.320
There are a few people.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.260
Oh, there are quite
a few people, great.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.780
How many of you are in the
provider space, in the hospital

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.090
space?

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:15.890
Got it.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:18.340
There are a few.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.200
If you look under the hood of
what's in a hospital system

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.750
or specifically behind an
electronic health record,

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:25.890
it is this.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:29.940
It's thousands of
tables, a lot of data,

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.280
mostly highly normalized,
very hard to find

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.170
any one-to-one relationships.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.890
And then when you compound that
with a problem like this, where

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:41.820
there is data everywhere
else, in fact, only about,

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.380
I would say 20%, 30% of the
data that we analyze and store

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.330
comes from the
electronic health record,

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.690
but there is everywhere else.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:50.820
It's outside the EMR.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.940
It's in our ERP systems,
in our networking systems.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.950
And believe it or
not, all the data

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:58.650
is still relevant
to health care.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:01.810
It is still relevant to how we
provide care to our patients.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:06.210
So we are very interested in
putting the data together,

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.300
putting it in a common platform,
and trying to solve health care

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.370
problems with the data.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.992
In short, health care
data is classic big data.

00:05:13.992 --> 00:05:15.450
It comes from all
different angles.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.550
It has veracity, it has
velocity, and you name it.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.030
So what are we doing with it?

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:23.070
Specifically, like I said,
the idea of this presentation

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.380
was to talk about
unstructured data,

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.630
and what are we doing
with unstructured data?

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.477
As you may know--

00:05:29.477 --> 00:05:31.560
because people specifically
who are in health care

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:34.143
and people who are specifically
working in the hospital field,

00:05:34.143 --> 00:05:37.320
you may know that out-of-box,
we don't necessarily

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.866
have solutions to easily
analyze health care data.

00:05:41.866 --> 00:05:47.000
An EHR doesn't come with
an NLP, so to say, right?

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.140
We would have to implement that.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:50.810
We would have to
extract the data.

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.850
We would have to manage the data
and implement a solution that

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.160
does analyze unstructured data.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.350
Doctors like to type notes.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.330
In a typical EHR
implementation, there

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:01.770
are more than 100-plus notes.

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:03.690
There are progress notes.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:04.920
There are pathology notes.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.360
There are nurses' notes.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:07.990
There are notes everywhere.

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:10.920
In fact, even in a
most simple statement,

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.690
like a nurse would
be typing and saying

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.390
does this person-- oh, I receive
a drug, like I'm saying there,

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:17.670
Sally is getting
drug from a friend.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:19.885
When you get that
medication ontology

00:06:19.885 --> 00:06:21.510
and you understand
what those drugs are

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.040
and how do they line up
to the patient's problems,

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:26.610
it's a very difficult problem
to solve because those insights

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:28.360
are not readily available.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:30.060
So what do we do with
unstructured data

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:31.227
to be able to solve problem?

00:06:31.227 --> 00:06:34.560
What are you doing today?

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.210
And like I said, it is not
really necessarily easy.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:42.630
And any solution that you read
about unstructured data or NLP

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:45.030
having done on health
care is mostly still,

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:47.940
today, living on
the research side.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:50.430
Not very many implementations
or not very many

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:53.480
solutions or products have
come on the commercial side,

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.370
and that's what Rush,
specifically our team

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:57.630
is focused on doing.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.243
I have some folks
from my team here

00:06:59.243 --> 00:07:00.660
who have been
living and breathing

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:02.930
NLP and unstructured data
for the last two years.

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:04.830
Guys, you want to raise
your hands, please?

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:05.770
Accolades to them.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.170
These are amazing engineers.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:09.960
They have learned so much
about medical ontologies,

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.520
so much about how to understand
what those ontologies are

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:14.910
and how they relate to
solving medical problems,

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:16.025
it's incredible.

00:07:16.025 --> 00:07:17.650
I encourage all of
you to talk to them.

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.620
I am just one person
representing all the work

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:23.520
that we have done over
two years with NLP data.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:26.970
So in short, it is very
difficult to extract the data.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:28.610
It is very difficult
to store the data

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:31.152
in a comprehensive solution,
and be able to provide solution.

00:07:31.152 --> 00:07:33.300
It is very, very
costly, in fact.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:37.110
Like I said, we have spent
a good two years, a good two

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:40.350
years to extract the data from
an EMR, store it in a format

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:42.270
that we can understand
in a single platform

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:44.680
and be able to analyze it.

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:47.180
What does the data
look like today?

00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:49.830
An average provider
note, specifically,

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:53.040
if you look at it is
about 45 kilobytes.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.940
And we collect about 8.5
million notes per year.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:58.410
If you do two years--

00:07:58.410 --> 00:07:59.630
when we started the project--

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:01.005
why am I saying
two years here is

00:08:01.005 --> 00:08:03.820
because when we started this
NLP project in our institution

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:07.530
first, our focus was to
look at at least two years

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:10.070
worth of notes, right?

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:12.540
Historically go back and
extract two years' worth of data

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:15.270
and analyze it and see
what insights we can find.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:18.390
And sure enough, just those
two years' worth of notes

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:22.640
were so big that our data
centers couldn't handle it.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:24.720
Or we were not able to
store it and process it

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.550
in a single infrastructure.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:28.050
So we had to look
at other solutions

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.460
to be able to do that.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:33.617
And now, because what we
achieved with finding insights,

00:08:33.617 --> 00:08:35.700
and I'm going to talk about
that in a few minutes,

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:38.850
with two years' worth of data,
the institution has asked us

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:42.210
now to expand that search,
go back to extracting 10

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:43.740
years' worth of data.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.500
And that literally will end
up in petabytes of data.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:47.995
It's really a problem to solve.

00:08:47.995 --> 00:08:49.620
How do we actually
analyze all the data

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.370
and be able to provide
meaningful insights

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:53.850
for the institution?

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:56.800
The use cases that
we specifically

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.050
started with for
unstructured data

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:04.120
is around adenoma,
as adenoma detection

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:05.500
is a very important measure.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:07.180
It's a CMS measure.

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:12.280
It gives our GI department--

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:14.980
if the doctors, for example,
if the patients come in

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:16.960
and they're not screening
the patients properly

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:20.540
in colonoscopy as for colon
cancer, it's a big deal.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:23.000
It's a measure that gets
measured by the CMS,

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:23.500
like I said.

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:27.250
So we started doing our
NLP with adenoma detection,

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:29.920
meaning we were extracting
all the pathology

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:32.530
and the provider notes
to be able to identify

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.493
where the doctors were
typing for adenomas.

00:09:34.493 --> 00:09:36.660
And we were able to provide
a dashboard straight off

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:39.400
of the data for them
to be able to see.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.220
We were successful in providing
a use case for adenoma, where

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:45.470
now providers are actively
using a dashboard off

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:50.200
of our unstructured data
in real time in memory

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:53.110
to be able to make decisions
about which patients to be

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:56.080
called back into the health care
or into the clinical pathway

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:58.940
to prevent people from
getting colon cancer.

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:00.560
So this is a big deal.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:03.010
It is a big deal both from
a regulatory point of view

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:05.050
for the institution
to be compliant

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:06.640
of a specific measure.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:09.235
It's a big deal for
patients, because we

00:10:09.235 --> 00:10:11.860
are in the business of
providing best health

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:13.160
care for our patients.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.580
The second was
about H&amp;P, history

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:17.440
and physical for the providers.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:20.840
History and physical is
also a regulatory measure.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:26.200
So to be specific
about it, so to explain

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:28.780
what it means for
people who may not know,

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:32.390
is if I am going in a
surgery, for example,

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:36.970
and if my history and physical
is not complete in my EHR 48

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:41.530
hours prior to the surgery, that
surgery needs to be canceled.

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:43.030
If any institution
fails to do that,

00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:44.355
then it's a bad mark on you.

00:10:44.355 --> 00:10:45.730
You can get audited,
and it would

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:47.570
be exception for institution.

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:50.320
So we cannot make that happen.

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:51.850
And for an institution
of our size

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:53.830
with three different
hospitals, making

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:56.950
sure every surgery has every
history and physical completed

00:10:56.950 --> 00:10:58.940
manually is a big deal.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:00.780
It actually required a
staff of three nurses

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:02.530
to go through all the
history and physical

00:11:02.530 --> 00:11:03.940
to make sure it's complete.

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:07.690
Well, we were able to use our
NLP engine, our AI platform,

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:10.480
to be able to
comprehensively identify

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:13.300
and score history and
physicals which were complete

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:14.472
and which were not complete.

00:11:14.472 --> 00:11:16.930
And the guy who did this here
is [? Darpin ?] [? Amin. ?] I

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:19.420
mean, [? Darpin ?] was
instrumental in figuring out

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:23.110
an AI solution, full end-to-end,
where we extract the histories

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:25.748
and physicals from our EMR,
we run it through our NLP,

00:11:25.748 --> 00:11:27.790
and we make sure that the
history and physical is

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:29.332
complete before we
provide it, again,

00:11:29.332 --> 00:11:32.982
dashboard with the end users
to be able to recall either

00:11:32.982 --> 00:11:35.190
history or physical are
completed before the process.

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:36.575
So it's a huge, huge case--

00:11:36.575 --> 00:11:38.200
amazingly valuable
for the institution.

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:40.540
We were able to save
time, money, and resources

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:41.620
for the institution.

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:44.350
And the third one,
last but not the least

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:46.330
is clinical documentation
improvement.

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:49.780
Like I was giving an example
of where very mundane notes can

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:52.060
be typed about medication,
about health conditions,

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:54.818
about comorbidities, they're
not readily available

00:11:54.818 --> 00:11:56.860
unless somebody is able
to go through those notes

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:58.732
and extract those insights.

00:11:58.732 --> 00:12:00.940
Clinical documentation
improvement is very important,

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:02.440
one, to make sure
that the patients,

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:05.410
we are able to provide better
health care, proper health

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:08.320
care, relevant health
care to the patients.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:11.050
And at the same time, again,
it helps the institution

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:13.480
to affect and address
the bottom line.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:15.670
That is, if you are
in the payer space,

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:17.030
you know what I'm talking about.

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:18.080
If you are in the
insurance space,

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:19.540
you know what I'm talking about.

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:22.550
If you send a claim for
the patient's record

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:25.060
or the transaction without
proper documentation,

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:26.350
then guess what?

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:27.100
It gets denied.

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:27.970
It gets rejected.

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:29.140
It gets delayed.

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.870
So it is extremely important
for us to be able to make sure

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:34.750
that the clinical documentation
improvement is in place.

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:37.890
And in short of having
everything completed manually,

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:40.210
we developed a solution
that is 100% based

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:42.820
on data extraction,
unstructured data

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:45.310
to improve our clinical
documentation improvement.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:47.140
All the use cases
that are talked about,

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:50.170
obviously, requires
both processing the data

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:53.140
in the back end, running
through an NLP engine,

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:56.530
matching with medical ontology,
sometimes general ontology,

00:12:56.530 --> 00:12:59.530
sometimes very specific
to specific service lines,

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:02.100
be it cancer, be it hip
and joint, what not.

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:04.120
And on top of that,
we have to make sure

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:07.180
that we are able to provide
the solutions to the end users

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:10.670
to be able to consume the
information that the engine has

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:11.590
provided, right?

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:13.750
Without that, it
becomes very difficult.

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:18.960
And all these are definitely
important problems,

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:20.980
but they are very hard problems.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:24.910
We often run into
people, vendors

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:27.220
who talk about having one
solution in one place,

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:29.020
and to this day, we
haven't found it.

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:31.030
It's very, very
difficult. You always

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:32.500
have to put pieces
together to be

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:35.200
able to solve the problem
with unstructured data.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:39.910
And anybody who is familiar
with working with file system

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:42.880
databases and extracting the
data in the native format,

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:44.840
storing it, it's a challenge.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:47.020
And if I'm doing
it on a daily basis

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:49.480
to operationalize
something, it's a challenge.

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:51.980
These problems are
definitely not easy to solve,

00:13:51.980 --> 00:13:54.090
but they are very,
very important.

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:56.630
And if anybody, in
my opinion, is not

00:13:56.630 --> 00:13:58.580
dealing with
unstructured data that

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:01.670
is available in the institution,
in any health care institution,

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:05.690
then we are not looking at
one of the key components

00:14:05.690 --> 00:14:07.250
of the data that
we need to analyze.

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:10.790
So the story that you need
to take away from today

00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:14.030
is that as an
institution, if you're not

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:16.460
tackling unstructured
data, then you're leaving

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:18.770
a big piece of puzzle behind.

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:21.440
It is very, very important
that institution like ours,

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:24.320
who are primarily responsible
in providing best health care,

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:28.740
should 100% look to
solve unstructured data,

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:30.875
analyze it, solve
it, and provide it

00:14:30.875 --> 00:14:33.800
as a solution for
the institutions.

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:35.910
Having said that, the
challenges are unlimited.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:39.020
There is many more use
cases that can be done.

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:41.520
Even today, after what we
have done for past two years,

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:44.113
we still have
disjoint data sets.

00:14:44.113 --> 00:14:45.530
There is unstructured
data, like I

00:14:45.530 --> 00:14:47.820
said, in our ERPs that
need to be brought back

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:48.750
that will be consumed.

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:50.480
There are all
types of solutions,

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:52.520
and we are slowly and
definitely working

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:54.750
towards solving those problems.

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:58.760
This slide specifically was to
help you understand what it is

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:00.500
and show you how
we have achieved

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:02.210
some of those use cases.

00:15:02.210 --> 00:15:04.740
What we wanted to do,
what we have learned--

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:07.880
definitely, to be able
to solve or put together

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:09.530
a solution using
unstructured data,

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:12.410
we have to make sure that we're
able to solve the problem, one,

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:14.450
by collaboration
internally and externally

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:17.160
with the partners and the
people within the resources.

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:18.900
We have to make sure
that we are able to,

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:21.025
like I said, to provide
notes to the end users that

00:15:21.025 --> 00:15:23.468
is intuitive for them to
be able to consume the data

00:15:23.468 --> 00:15:25.760
and be able to work with the
data that we have provided

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:27.290
and the insights.

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:29.133
How do they do
interventions with the data

00:15:29.133 --> 00:15:30.050
that we are providing?

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:33.620
What actions they are
able to take with the data

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:34.610
you're providing.

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:38.208
These things cannot
be isolated or siloed.

00:15:38.208 --> 00:15:40.250
You have to put that
together as you are thinking

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:41.760
through these problems.

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:43.580
The second is scale.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:45.030
How do we scale this problem?

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:47.910
How do we, like I was saying,
go from two years to 10 years?

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:52.070
And how do we do it in a
cost-effective fashion, right?

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:53.750
And single source of truth.

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:55.140
This continues to be a problem.

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:57.050
We have tried so many
different solutions.

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:59.690
And I can speak to you
very transparently,

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:02.630
that when we first started on
this journey two years ago,

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:05.405
four or five months within
the project, we failed.

00:16:05.405 --> 00:16:08.030
We miserably failed
because we were not

00:16:08.030 --> 00:16:09.800
able to properly
implement a solution that

00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:11.540
was cost effective.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:14.810
The costs that we were incurring
for storing and processing

00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:17.060
the data was growing
significantly.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:18.847
There was a question
about why not do it,

00:16:18.847 --> 00:16:20.930
and then how was I supposed
to do it in the cloud?

00:16:20.930 --> 00:16:23.388
There were so many different
problems that we had to solve.

00:16:23.388 --> 00:16:27.320
So it's definitely worth trying
something, failing early,

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:28.820
and fixing it as you go about.

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:30.360
That's the lesson
that we learned

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:32.870
and wanted to share with you.

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:33.452
What now?

00:16:33.452 --> 00:16:34.160
So what do we do?

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:36.568
Like I said, we want to go
into the space of pop health.

00:16:36.568 --> 00:16:39.110
We want to be able to do a lot
of KPI performance improvement

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:40.465
using this data.

00:16:40.465 --> 00:16:41.840
We have a lot
novel cases that we

00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:44.990
want to implement
as we move forward.

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:46.700
Maybe this is time
for us to talk

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:50.570
about how we are partnering
with Google and Maven Wave

00:16:50.570 --> 00:16:52.640
to sort of solve this problem.

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:56.150
Like I said, unstructured data
obviously is very important

00:16:56.150 --> 00:17:00.640
to solve, but you cannot solve
that before actually also

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:02.630
mating that with
the structured data.

00:17:02.630 --> 00:17:05.983
And how we are using or
leveraging GCP today,

00:17:05.983 --> 00:17:07.400
I think Vasu will
talk about that.

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:08.050
Vasu, please.

00:17:08.050 --> 00:17:08.619
Yes.

00:17:08.619 --> 00:17:09.770
VASUDHA GUPTA: All right.

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:11.480
Thanks, Jawad.

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:14.599
So Jawad hit on kind
of the business problem

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:17.690
that we were really looking
at at Rush and really kind

00:17:17.690 --> 00:17:21.410
of this unique problem of
melding the unstructured data

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:22.460
with structured data.

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:24.200
What I'm going to talk
a little bit about

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:27.920
is really how Google Cloud
Healthcare and the products

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:31.490
and solutions that we offer
was instrumental in making

00:17:31.490 --> 00:17:34.280
this kind of business problem
and solving this business

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:35.090
problem.

00:17:35.090 --> 00:17:37.130
And really the focus of my--

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:39.295
or the focus of my part
of the presentation

00:17:39.295 --> 00:17:42.770
will really be on the Cloud
Healthcare API and the Clinical

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:45.650
Data Warehouse, which were two
of the unique products that

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:48.770
were critical and integral
to really building out

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:52.490
this end-to-end pipeline for
solving the business problem.

00:17:52.490 --> 00:17:56.600
And Cloud Healthcare API
being that key mechanism

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:59.450
for ingesting and storing
and analyzing of the data

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:01.160
and then clinical
data warehouse as you

00:18:01.160 --> 00:18:03.590
look at different sources and
all of these disparate data

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:06.800
formats coming together,
how you really kind of put

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:08.540
all that together
and really build

00:18:08.540 --> 00:18:11.600
this notion of a clinical
data warehouse sitting

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:13.520
on top of something
like BigQuery

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:18.790
for the petabytes amount of data
that you're looking to handle

00:18:18.790 --> 00:18:21.680
So what is the Cloud
Healthcare API?

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:25.820
So it's a solution that's being
built by the Cloud Healthcare

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:29.810
Team that really speaks,
understands, talks the health

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:33.230
care-specific vernacular and
health care-specific language.

00:18:33.230 --> 00:18:37.410
It's really meant to accelerate
your ingestion of your data,

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:40.760
your storage of the data,
the analysis of the data,

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:42.920
specific to health care.

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:44.900
In its current
state right now, it

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:49.100
supports DICOM,
HL7v2, and FHIR, which

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:51.790
stands for Fast Healthcare
Interoperability Resources.

00:18:51.790 --> 00:18:54.060
And since there are so many
people in the crowd that

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:55.560
are from the health
care industry,

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:56.925
this should not be a surprise.

00:18:56.925 --> 00:18:59.300
And if you look at sort of a
trend within the health care

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:01.758
industry and the fact that
there are so many disparate data

00:19:01.758 --> 00:19:05.510
formats, there is kind of a move
in the industry in health care

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:07.820
to really standardize
to adopting

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:09.620
FHIR as that gold standard.

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:11.480
But like I said,
the Healthcare API

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:13.610
supports all these
different three data

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:18.560
types in its native form
within the Healthcare API.

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:21.930
What does the Healthcare API
comprise of in a nutshell?

00:19:21.930 --> 00:19:25.280
So really, I kind of boil it
down to three main components.

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:27.200
At the center of
it all is really

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:30.350
a secure, high-performant
repository

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:33.410
that exists for DICOM,
HL7v2, and FHIR.

00:19:33.410 --> 00:19:36.980
So there's a FHIR
store, an HL7v2 store,

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:40.490
and a DICOM store that exists
within the Healthcare API.

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:42.530
And then wrapped
around that repository

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:46.190
is a series of both
admin and access APIs

00:19:46.190 --> 00:19:48.770
that are compliant with
health care data standards

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:51.335
and really allow you
to talk that health

00:19:51.335 --> 00:19:53.030
care-specific
language and access

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:55.670
that data that's
sitting in those stores.

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:59.000
And then third is really a suite
of integrations and capability

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:02.100
that allow you to do the most
with your health care data.

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:05.480
So a combination of the
repositories, the APIs,

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:07.010
and the integrations,
whether it be

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:09.020
integrations to
your systems that

00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:13.460
is ingesting the
data or integrations

00:20:13.460 --> 00:20:16.500
outside to external applications
to send the data out,

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:18.890
Healthcare API provides
those capabilities

00:20:18.890 --> 00:20:21.860
and that's why it's a unique
and compelling product

00:20:21.860 --> 00:20:24.380
and also why it was
critical in kind

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:26.510
of solving this
problem of ingesting

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:29.330
this unstructured data
which, in this case for Rush,

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:32.660
was within FHIR messages that we
ingested from the different EMR

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:34.860
systems.

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:37.970
So if you kind of look at
the clinical data mapping

00:20:37.970 --> 00:20:41.120
workflow in its kind
of entirety, the data,

00:20:41.120 --> 00:20:43.140
like I said, it could
be in different formats.

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:45.380
It can be in HL7v2, FHIR.

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:48.440
It can also be in some of the
other non-standard formats,

00:20:48.440 --> 00:20:50.730
like CSV, XML, so and so forth.

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:53.030
So as you're looking
to ingest these data

00:20:53.030 --> 00:20:56.300
across from different
systems and different formats

00:20:56.300 --> 00:20:59.810
and really trying to build
this sort of central place

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:03.740
in storing that data, you kind
of go to the Healthcare API.

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:07.430
And like I said, there's a FHIR
store within the Healthcare API

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:09.930
that allows you to
capture that data.

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:12.770
But there's like a middle
transformation layer, right?

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:14.540
So you've got all these
different sources,

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:16.370
and especially if
you're looking at doing

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:19.730
cross-modality analysis across
the different data sources,

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:24.300
that transformation layer to
convert HL7 to FHIR or CSV

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:26.390
to FHIR, really allowing
that and capturing

00:21:26.390 --> 00:21:28.660
that data in something
like a FHIR store

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:31.280
is that something that Cloud
Healthcare API does really

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:32.100
well.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:34.610
And then once the data is
there and the Cloud Healthcare

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:37.700
API in that FHIR store,
the data can be exposed

00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:39.660
to external applications.

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:42.290
So an example could be
an application sitting

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:44.900
on top of App Engine
or even the data

00:21:44.900 --> 00:21:47.030
could be exposed as
APIs through something

00:21:47.030 --> 00:21:48.990
like Apigee and so and so forth.

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:51.410
So that data, again,
can be exposed

00:21:51.410 --> 00:21:53.340
to external applications.

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:55.730
And then also, if you're
looking at, again,

00:21:55.730 --> 00:21:58.940
melding that unstructured and
structured data, if you want

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:01.700
that structured analysis of
that data-- in this case,

00:22:01.700 --> 00:22:04.670
we had notes that we were
ingesting as FHIR messages,

00:22:04.670 --> 00:22:08.310
and we wanted to create a schema
that was standard for analysis.

00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:11.330
The data can also be stored
in something like BigQuery.

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:14.330
Or even, if you want to
do machine learning models

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:17.345
on top of the data, than
really using a product

00:22:17.345 --> 00:22:19.970
like cloud and meld engine to do
some of that machine learning.

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:21.387
So all in all, if
you kind of look

00:22:21.387 --> 00:22:24.230
at that end-to-end
workflow, from the ingestion

00:22:24.230 --> 00:22:26.743
to the standardization
to really exposing

00:22:26.743 --> 00:22:28.160
the data in different
ways, that's

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:31.760
something that we built as the
common architecture pattern

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:34.730
when we were working with Rush.

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:37.550
One of the other capabilities
that the Healthcare

00:22:37.550 --> 00:22:41.400
API provides is the ability to
react to data near real time.

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:43.130
And what I mean
by that is there's

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:47.150
a strong integration between the
Cloud Healthcare API and Cloud

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:51.650
Pub/Sub, which is our real-time
messaging and event streaming

00:22:51.650 --> 00:22:52.470
service.

00:22:52.470 --> 00:22:57.740
And anytime a data lands in the
FHIR store or the DICOM store,

00:22:57.740 --> 00:22:59.960
it essentially sends
a notification message

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:00.950
to Pub/Sub.

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:03.470
And that could be a trigger
for different things

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:06.710
that you do downstream,
again, whether it be rendering

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:10.850
to certain applications or maybe
a cloud functions routine that,

00:23:10.850 --> 00:23:14.000
in turn, executes a
machine learning model,

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.130
the output of which can
then be integrated back

00:23:16.130 --> 00:23:18.680
into your external application.

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:21.050
So this kind of
powerful notion of being

00:23:21.050 --> 00:23:23.570
able to react to data
real time, to think

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:26.300
about real-time inferences
as you start building

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:30.140
your machine learning models
is a really compelling story

00:23:30.140 --> 00:23:32.360
with the Healthcare API.

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:34.025
Another capability
that is provided

00:23:34.025 --> 00:23:37.810
is the ability to de-identify
your FHIR and DICOM resources.

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:40.030
So within the Cloud
Healthcare API,

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:42.530
there is a de-identify
call that you can make.

00:23:42.530 --> 00:23:46.010
And depending on the
attributes that you specify,

00:23:46.010 --> 00:23:47.480
you can configure
that and it will

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:49.550
de-identify those
specific attributes

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:53.190
and create essentially another
version of your data set

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:56.670
or store and capture that within
another FHIR store or DICOM

00:23:56.670 --> 00:23:57.920
store, and so on and so forth.

00:23:57.920 --> 00:23:59.378
And this is
applicable, again, even

00:23:59.378 --> 00:24:02.740
on the DICOM images,
the FHIR resources,

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:04.780
and so on and so forth.

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:07.680
And then last but not least,
another key capability

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:10.440
is the ability to export
this FHIR and DICOM

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:12.270
metadata to BigQuery.

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:15.870
So like, again, coming
back to Jawad's point,

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:18.660
this notion of really
being able to analyze

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:20.820
this unstructured data
and make sense of it

00:24:20.820 --> 00:24:23.370
and capturing it in
something like BigQuery

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:25.170
can be extremely powerful.

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:26.830
One of the things
that's not on here

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:28.872
but that's something that's
being released really

00:24:28.872 --> 00:24:30.990
soon is Cloud
Healthcare API is also

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:33.720
going to be able to support
real-time streaming of data

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:34.470
to BigQuery.

00:24:34.470 --> 00:24:36.180
So that's also coming.

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:38.280
So in the future,
essentially, as the data

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:42.630
is coming to Healthcare API,
without this export call,

00:24:42.630 --> 00:24:45.820
the data can be streamed
into BigQuery directly.

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:49.530
So again, kind of in summary,
this Cloud Healthcare API

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:52.170
was central and core to
building this pipeline

00:24:52.170 --> 00:24:55.650
and ingesting these FHIR,
these unstructured data

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:58.530
and the clinical notes
embedded in the FHIR messages

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:00.870
for our solution.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:02.790
But kind of shifting
gears a little bit

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:05.550
and talking more on the
structured data side,

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:08.430
again, if you think about
going back to this idea

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:11.250
that you have data coming
from different sources

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:15.060
and different formats,
both historical data

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:17.400
and incremental data,
you want to map it

00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:19.327
to some sort of a common
data model, right?

00:25:19.327 --> 00:25:20.910
There's a lot of
different data models

00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:23.150
that exist within the
health care industry.

00:25:23.150 --> 00:25:26.340
So thinking about kind of your
OMOP data models, CDISC or even

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:29.460
a consistent and structured
FHIR schema that,

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:32.100
again, is that middle layer,
the mapping and transformation

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:33.370
layer that becomes critical.

00:25:33.370 --> 00:25:35.850
So how do I map HL7 to FHIR?

00:25:35.850 --> 00:25:38.070
Or how do I map CSV to FHIR?

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:41.160
So one of the other products
that the Google Health

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:43.500
Team is investing
heavily in is really

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:45.510
building this mapping
and transformation layer

00:25:45.510 --> 00:25:49.650
to make this Clinical Data
Warehouse solution really

00:25:49.650 --> 00:25:51.780
effective and really efficient.

00:25:51.780 --> 00:25:53.500
And the idea behind
that is going

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:57.030
to be building of a mapping
and a configuration tool that

00:25:57.030 --> 00:25:59.940
allows you to map between
different data formats

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:02.790
and provides you
this UI and GUI-like

00:26:02.790 --> 00:26:05.250
interface to allow you to map
between different formats.

00:26:05.250 --> 00:26:07.680
And we're interjecting
that layer

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:10.000
with a lot of machine
learning capabilities.

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:13.530
So how does that mapping
layer become smarter over time

00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:17.490
as you look to transform and
build these target schemas

00:26:17.490 --> 00:26:20.700
and build this notion of
a clinical data warehouse

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:22.350
on BigQuery?

00:26:22.350 --> 00:26:25.440
And kind of taking
it one level further,

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:29.045
if you think about what you
really want to do from--

00:26:29.045 --> 00:26:30.670
you've got all these
different sources.

00:26:30.670 --> 00:26:32.545
You've got all these
different types of data.

00:26:32.545 --> 00:26:34.480
How do I really do
cross-modality analysis?

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:37.770
How do I blend, for
instance, maybe genomics data

00:26:37.770 --> 00:26:42.180
in the future to the data that's
coming from FHIR, HL7, DICOM,

00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:43.230
so and so forth?

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:45.510
So again, thinking
about the pipeline

00:26:45.510 --> 00:26:47.790
ingesting the data
from EHR systems,

00:26:47.790 --> 00:26:51.720
or your PAC systems into the
Healthcare AI specific stores,

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:54.750
capturing that data in
BigQuery, performing

00:26:54.750 --> 00:26:57.630
certain transforms and
different analytics on it,

00:26:57.630 --> 00:27:01.410
and really creating these
ready-to-consume schemas, which

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:04.590
could be, again, OMOPs,
custom-made schemas,

00:27:04.590 --> 00:27:06.930
FHIR schemas, so and
so forth, and having it

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:10.230
to the end user for different
analytics and visualization use

00:27:10.230 --> 00:27:12.810
cases is really
our kind of vision

00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:16.620
for this cross-modality
analysis and really this ability

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:19.140
to build this clinical
data warehouse in BigQuery.

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:21.930
So I'll just wrap up by saying
that, again, Cloud Healthcare

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:24.450
API and the Clinical
Data Warehouse

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:28.500
were key health care products
and solutions that we

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:31.817
kind of considered as we were
building this architecture.

00:27:31.817 --> 00:27:33.400
And I'm going to
hand it over to Dave,

00:27:33.400 --> 00:27:35.442
who's going to talk about
the end-to-end solution

00:27:35.442 --> 00:27:36.040
architecture.

00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:36.980
DAVID PATTERSON: Thank you.

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:37.480
Vasu.

00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:40.580
All right.

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:43.130
So I get to, hopefully,
bring some of the solution

00:27:43.130 --> 00:27:46.670
together and link the
Healthcare API back

00:27:46.670 --> 00:27:50.410
to the message and challenges
that we were having at Rush.

00:27:50.410 --> 00:27:53.720
So what I want to show
you is how we can combine

00:27:53.720 --> 00:27:57.800
the Healthcare API, along with
a natural language processing

00:27:57.800 --> 00:28:01.490
engine running on GCP to
unlock key health care

00:28:01.490 --> 00:28:03.720
use cases that
Jawad referred to.

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:08.030
That includes document integrity
checking, clinical decision

00:28:08.030 --> 00:28:12.620
support, public health
records, and disease trends.

00:28:12.620 --> 00:28:15.660
Before I get to the
solution, I wanted

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:17.930
to give you or share
with you the journey

00:28:17.930 --> 00:28:22.112
that Maven Wave and
Rush has been on.

00:28:22.112 --> 00:28:23.570
When Maven Wave
works with clients,

00:28:23.570 --> 00:28:26.210
we typically
recommend four phases

00:28:26.210 --> 00:28:28.730
for moving workloads to GCP.

00:28:28.730 --> 00:28:30.300
The first one is assess.

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:32.055
So this is when
you're prototyping,

00:28:32.055 --> 00:28:33.680
you're experimenting,
and really trying

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:37.140
to figure out if GCP is the
right fit for your solution.

00:28:37.140 --> 00:28:38.630
The second is planning.

00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:40.310
This is setting
up infrastructure,

00:28:40.310 --> 00:28:42.560
getting projects
organized, setting up

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:48.080
billing, and most importantly,
picking your first workload.

00:28:48.080 --> 00:28:50.720
Moving on to deployment-- this
is implementation, migration,

00:28:50.720 --> 00:28:53.090
of the workload, and
finally operationalizing,

00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:56.510
which is standing up a program
with your kind of future work

00:28:56.510 --> 00:28:57.680
backlog.

00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:02.240
Key throughout this process
is to tightly align scope

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:04.430
to business value
and security needs.

00:29:04.430 --> 00:29:07.880
Rather than trying to build the
perfect technology platform,

00:29:07.880 --> 00:29:11.210
really kind of quickly
deliver business value

00:29:11.210 --> 00:29:14.960
to build credibility
in the organization.

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:16.550
So where we're at
with Rush-- we are

00:29:16.550 --> 00:29:19.910
through the first deployment
and working with them

00:29:19.910 --> 00:29:23.463
to define future workloads that
Jawad will talk through here

00:29:23.463 --> 00:29:24.130
in a little bit.

00:29:26.700 --> 00:29:27.510
All right.

00:29:27.510 --> 00:29:30.040
So let's get on to
document coding basics.

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:32.040
So back to what Jawad
was referring to

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:36.090
in terms of getting structure
out of unstructured patient

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:36.610
documents.

00:29:36.610 --> 00:29:38.740
So what are the basic
building blocks?

00:29:38.740 --> 00:29:41.550
The first one is
applying core NLP.

00:29:41.550 --> 00:29:43.710
And this is kind of the
standard technologies that

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:45.840
are used across natural
language technologies,

00:29:45.840 --> 00:29:50.160
but that includes parts
of speech extraction,

00:29:50.160 --> 00:29:54.750
context, negation detection, and
most importantly for this use

00:29:54.750 --> 00:29:57.630
case is the named
entity recognition.

00:29:57.630 --> 00:30:00.960
That's a challenge in
medical or clinical notes,

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:03.510
which makes the
second step critical,

00:30:03.510 --> 00:30:05.742
which is applying
medical vocabularies.

00:30:05.742 --> 00:30:07.200
And these are
vocabularies that are

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:10.080
curated by international
health organizations.

00:30:10.080 --> 00:30:14.460
The two that we used in
this solution was SNOMED CT.

00:30:14.460 --> 00:30:17.830
That includes 300,000
coded medical concepts,

00:30:17.830 --> 00:30:21.360
including diagnoses, clinical
findings, body structures,

00:30:21.360 --> 00:30:22.740
and observations.

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:26.970
The second is RxNORM,
which is geared

00:30:26.970 --> 00:30:28.440
towards pharmaceuticals
and links

00:30:28.440 --> 00:30:30.400
to common drug vocabularies.

00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:33.450
So the third building block
is taking our coded documents,

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:36.330
and like Vasu was mentioning,
getting them stored

00:30:36.330 --> 00:30:40.160
in a clinical data warehouse.

00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:44.300
So let's look at an example
of what this looks like.

00:30:44.300 --> 00:30:46.790
Here, you can see
the precise codings

00:30:46.790 --> 00:30:49.550
that have been extracted
from a fragment

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:51.560
of a clinical narrative.

00:30:51.560 --> 00:30:53.510
And it's these
precise codings that

00:30:53.510 --> 00:30:58.550
really form the foundation
for cross-patient analytics.

00:31:02.860 --> 00:31:03.360
All right.

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:06.210
So in terms of our
solution approach, first,

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:08.880
we wanted to land raw,
unstructured patient data

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:10.110
in the Healthcare FHIR store.

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:14.730
So we didn't use a great
amount of the data structures

00:31:14.730 --> 00:31:18.510
available in the API, but we
did get a lot of acceleration

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:21.570
just from using and not
having to redefine a schema

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:24.770
as we're moving raw data
from the EMR into Google.

00:31:24.770 --> 00:31:26.280
Second were the
security controls

00:31:26.280 --> 00:31:28.440
that were already in place.

00:31:28.440 --> 00:31:31.753
Second step was establish
an autoscaling medical NLP

00:31:31.753 --> 00:31:32.670
microservice platform.

00:31:32.670 --> 00:31:35.100
We did that on
Kubernetes engine.

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:37.530
Third was leveraging data
flow and beam pipelines

00:31:37.530 --> 00:31:39.143
for data orchestration.

00:31:39.143 --> 00:31:41.310
And then finally, creating
a clinical data warehouse

00:31:41.310 --> 00:31:41.880
in BigQuery.

00:31:44.790 --> 00:31:47.640
So establishing the
NLP core, fortunately,

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:50.610
there's been a lot of good
work, I think, Jawad mentioned,

00:31:50.610 --> 00:31:53.640
in the research hospitals,
research organizations,

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:55.510
around this problem set.

00:31:55.510 --> 00:31:57.790
So we were able to use two
open-source technologies,

00:31:57.790 --> 00:32:02.340
CTAKES and QuickULMS, and then
augment that with some custom

00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:05.716
rule-based NLP for Rush.

00:32:05.716 --> 00:32:08.970
The bad news is that some
of these technologies

00:32:08.970 --> 00:32:11.940
have evolved over the
course of a decade or more.

00:32:11.940 --> 00:32:13.680
So they're monolithic.

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:16.080
They had varied
support for APIs.

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:18.540
So the second step was to
modernize and normalize

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:20.220
those applications.

00:32:20.220 --> 00:32:23.010
So we performed
some optimizations

00:32:23.010 --> 00:32:26.280
and threading
around performance,

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:28.860
and then we implemented
web-friendly APIs

00:32:28.860 --> 00:32:31.080
in JSON and GRPC.

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:35.710
Finally, containerizing and then
moving to Kubernetes engine.

00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:39.840
And this was key to providing
that kind of autoscaling

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:42.540
capability and cost control
that Rush was looking for.

00:32:45.570 --> 00:32:47.990
So putting it all together--

00:32:47.990 --> 00:32:51.080
so working from the left, we
sourced unstructured patient

00:32:51.080 --> 00:32:53.390
records from the EMR.

00:32:53.390 --> 00:32:56.570
We landed them in
the Healthcare API.

00:32:56.570 --> 00:32:59.750
We leveraged some of the event
triggering that Vasu mentioned

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:03.560
to trigger real-time data
flows, to then apply the NLP

00:33:03.560 --> 00:33:06.740
coding, and finally, storing
that in a scalable data

00:33:06.740 --> 00:33:09.140
warehouse built on BigQuery.

00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:12.230
And that sets us up nicely for
future ML workloads as well as

00:33:12.230 --> 00:33:17.070
descriptive analytics
and data exploration.

00:33:17.070 --> 00:33:20.910
Some future workloads-- we
plan to take imaging data

00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:23.370
from the PAC system
DICOM files and move them

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:27.510
to the Healthcare API,
claims data and ERP also

00:33:27.510 --> 00:33:31.110
to kind of round out operations
and kind of do cross-analytics

00:33:31.110 --> 00:33:32.170
with that patient data.

00:33:34.810 --> 00:33:38.300
Here's a different view
of the same architecture.

00:33:38.300 --> 00:33:42.010
We can see EMR, some of
the future data sources

00:33:42.010 --> 00:33:45.460
at Rush, patient
data storage in FHIR,

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:47.500
filtering and
parsing in data flow.

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:50.052
We've got a medical NLP
on Kubernetes engine.

00:33:50.052 --> 00:33:51.760
Finally, moving that
into a Clinical Data

00:33:51.760 --> 00:33:53.430
Warehouse on BigQuery.

00:33:57.260 --> 00:33:57.760
All right.

00:33:57.760 --> 00:33:59.470
I think I'm going
to hand it back over

00:33:59.470 --> 00:34:02.923
to Jawad for some takeaways
and road map items.

00:34:02.923 --> 00:34:04.090
JAWAD KHAN: Thank you, Dave.

00:34:04.090 --> 00:34:06.490
So like Vasu and
Dave mentioned, we

00:34:06.490 --> 00:34:09.130
started with just looking
at unstructured data,

00:34:09.130 --> 00:34:12.280
meaning we're specifically
focusing on just the notes

00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:13.969
that we were extracting.

00:34:13.969 --> 00:34:17.080
But there is several
modalities that we are now

00:34:17.080 --> 00:34:18.969
starting to work
on because we have

00:34:18.969 --> 00:34:22.880
confidence the architecture
seems to be pretty stable.

00:34:22.880 --> 00:34:25.270
And I think they really do
expand to other modalities.

00:34:25.270 --> 00:34:26.020
And the first one
that we're going

00:34:26.020 --> 00:34:28.060
to look at, as like
Dave talked about,

00:34:28.060 --> 00:34:32.350
is to connect our PAC server
to the GCP environment

00:34:32.350 --> 00:34:35.830
and be able to directly query
the data from the PAC server

00:34:35.830 --> 00:34:39.010
into a DICOM store and
then have machine learning

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:41.290
models run on that imaging.

00:34:41.290 --> 00:34:45.100
The second use case is to do
very similar to PAC server

00:34:45.100 --> 00:34:46.960
but more focus on
the cancer side,

00:34:46.960 --> 00:34:49.179
to actually do some mammograms.

00:34:49.179 --> 00:34:51.310
And there is a solution
already out there

00:34:51.310 --> 00:34:54.130
that we use today,
which is on-prem to do

00:34:54.130 --> 00:34:57.760
a real-time AI on a mammogram.

00:34:57.760 --> 00:34:59.900
So if somebody comes
in for a mammogram,

00:34:59.900 --> 00:35:01.600
the image gets into
the PAC server,

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:03.970
and we immediately run
it through a ML model

00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:06.520
to sort of annotate
it to see if it

00:35:06.520 --> 00:35:09.490
is positive/negative for
breast cancer, right?

00:35:09.490 --> 00:35:11.680
Those cases are running
today, on-prem today,

00:35:11.680 --> 00:35:14.660
but it's very isolated
to just mammograms.

00:35:14.660 --> 00:35:17.890
But we are expanding it to
other imaging solutions.

00:35:17.890 --> 00:35:20.470
And the idea is to be able
to do that over the cloud

00:35:20.470 --> 00:35:23.470
because obviously, it would have
to scale for other use cases.

00:35:23.470 --> 00:35:27.670
The third is our
clinical data model today

00:35:27.670 --> 00:35:31.180
is completely in
our data centers,

00:35:31.180 --> 00:35:34.780
in our on-prem data centers.

00:35:34.780 --> 00:35:37.660
Both the transactional
and operational databases,

00:35:37.660 --> 00:35:43.150
the data warehouses are all
within our data centers today

00:35:43.150 --> 00:35:45.160
and our unstructured
or advanced analytics

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:46.780
is all happening in cloud.

00:35:46.780 --> 00:35:48.460
So there is a sort
of a disconnect.

00:35:48.460 --> 00:35:50.620
Every time we have to
bring clinical data

00:35:50.620 --> 00:35:54.720
to be able to correlate with
our advanced analytic solutions,

00:35:54.720 --> 00:35:57.850
we are having to either
batch those queries back

00:35:57.850 --> 00:36:01.232
into the cloud or bring some
of the unstructured data

00:36:01.232 --> 00:36:03.190
that we have now converted
into structured data

00:36:03.190 --> 00:36:05.920
down into where
the warehouses are

00:36:05.920 --> 00:36:07.690
and be able to do analytics.

00:36:07.690 --> 00:36:09.647
But ideally, there is--

00:36:09.647 --> 00:36:11.230
I think the time is
right for us to be

00:36:11.230 --> 00:36:13.647
able to look at those data
structures and data warehousing

00:36:13.647 --> 00:36:15.330
solutions very carefully now.

00:36:15.330 --> 00:36:17.800
And we are seriously
giving it a very hard look

00:36:17.800 --> 00:36:21.550
to see if we can implement a
common clinical data model that

00:36:21.550 --> 00:36:25.420
supports both advanced analytics
and our VI solutions in cloud

00:36:25.420 --> 00:36:26.650
type of structure.

00:36:26.650 --> 00:36:31.300
There are not many very
well-designed clinical data

00:36:31.300 --> 00:36:32.480
models that are out there.

00:36:32.480 --> 00:36:35.070
We use-- I don't know if you
guys are familiar with OMOP.

00:36:35.070 --> 00:36:38.080
We use OMOP as a data model
for some of the use cases,

00:36:38.080 --> 00:36:40.840
but it doesn't necessarily
support outside

00:36:40.840 --> 00:36:43.000
of just transactions, right?

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:44.530
So we have other
clinical use cases

00:36:44.530 --> 00:36:47.380
that we want to expand
the data models to.

00:36:47.380 --> 00:36:49.100
And we really want a
true representation

00:36:49.100 --> 00:36:51.070
of the clinical data
model looks like,

00:36:51.070 --> 00:36:53.830
including the patient finances,
including corporate finances,

00:36:53.830 --> 00:36:55.477
and including our
clinical outcomes.

00:36:55.477 --> 00:36:57.310
We are very closely
looking at, like I said,

00:36:57.310 --> 00:37:00.560
to move that into our GCP or
BigQuery type of environment

00:37:00.560 --> 00:37:03.280
to be able to leverage both
structured and unstructured

00:37:03.280 --> 00:37:04.100
data.

00:37:04.100 --> 00:37:06.550
Last but not the least,
it is of high interest,

00:37:06.550 --> 00:37:09.250
I mean, it's one of the
institutional priorities

00:37:09.250 --> 00:37:11.590
to be able to develop
a search solution.

00:37:11.590 --> 00:37:14.800
Today, it is very difficult
to search within the EHRs,

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:16.270
electronic health records.

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:18.610
You can probably
do a search, even

00:37:18.610 --> 00:37:22.210
with a third-party solution
across the patient charts

00:37:22.210 --> 00:37:26.500
or maybe just looking at the
patient chart vertically,

00:37:26.500 --> 00:37:28.660
you're able to search
for comorbidities

00:37:28.660 --> 00:37:30.160
and some of the
diseases that you're

00:37:30.160 --> 00:37:31.600
looking for specifically.

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:35.630
But doing across the EMR
is a bit not there yet.

00:37:35.630 --> 00:37:39.460
So the provider is not able
to look his or her enpanelment

00:37:39.460 --> 00:37:42.310
in a very comprehensive way to
be able to identify solutions.

00:37:42.310 --> 00:37:45.190
And then to make
it more complex,

00:37:45.190 --> 00:37:47.500
what if you have
more than one EHR?

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:50.070
How are you then able to
search across all the EHRs

00:37:50.070 --> 00:37:52.340
if the provider is
seeing a patient, say,

00:37:52.340 --> 00:37:55.220
for example, at our satellite
location and a different EHR.

00:37:55.220 --> 00:37:58.008
And the provider, now comes back
to the hospital as an attendee.

00:37:58.008 --> 00:37:59.050
He has this patient here.

00:37:59.050 --> 00:38:01.630
He's not able to really
search comprehensively

00:38:01.630 --> 00:38:02.600
across all system.

00:38:02.600 --> 00:38:04.990
So the idea is to, again,
bring all this data

00:38:04.990 --> 00:38:08.950
together in a common clinical
data model, have unstructured

00:38:08.950 --> 00:38:11.590
data insights available
in a single place

00:38:11.590 --> 00:38:14.160
where we can do searches.

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:15.225
We have a name for it.

00:38:15.225 --> 00:38:16.600
We are calling it
single BigQuery

00:38:16.600 --> 00:38:19.150
that we want to implement
as part of the EHR solution.

00:38:19.150 --> 00:38:21.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

